begin  quoting  David Tillotson  <dave@atillo.com.au>:
>The point is, I thought the discussion was whether the fastest ascension 
>trophy should _require_ wishless conduct to reduce lucky breaks. My 
>argument is that many competitions that are considered to be tests of 
>skill can still be tipped by luck when equally skilled competitors face 
>off. Why should this matter just in the case of Nethack?

I think it matters here because early speed boots are so enormously
significant a factor in a fastest ascension.

Obviously in any contest of luck and skill, people's placements will be to
some degree related to their luck and to some degree related to their
skill. However, we might prefer contests where the placement is primarily
a matter of skill, rather than dominated by luck; for example, games like
bridge don't hang on the luck of one deal, but consist of several deals to
allow the players' skill to be a more important factor.

Personally, however, I think the answer is for the "moves" reported at
game end to be based on "opportunities to move if unburdened". This timer
would continue to tick away if the character was paralysed, asleep, etc.,
but hasting effects would provide only their (considerable) in-game
advantage, not a reduction in the "moves" reported.
